\title{Irreducibility of mod $p$ Reductions of Crystalline\\ Representations at Fractional Slope and Even Weight}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-12}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Let $p$ be an odd prime, $k \ge 2$ an even integer, and $a_p \in \Qpbar$ with
valuation $v(a_p) > 0$. When $v(a_p)$ is \emph{not} an integer we say the crystalline
representation $V_{k,a_p}$ of $\GQp$ has \emph{fractional slope}. A folklore
conjecture predicts that the semisimplified mod $p$ reduction $\bar V_{k,a_p}$ is
then irreducible. We isolate and prove the arithmetic engine behind this conjecture.
The Frobenius eigenvalues of $V_{k,a_p}$ are the roots of $X^2 - a_p X + p^{k-1}$;
normalising $v(p)=1$, their valuations (the \emph{Frobenius slopes}) are $v(a_p)$ and
$(k-1) - v(a_p)$ whenever $v(a_p) < (k-1)/2$. We prove that these slopes sum to
$k-1$, that non-integrality propagates from the low slope to the high slope, that
they are strictly ordered below the balanced point, and that for even $k$ the
balanced slope $(k-1)/2$ is a genuine half-integer. We complement this
valuation-theoretic layer with a linear-algebra layer valid over any field of
characteristic $\neq 2$: the polynomial $X^2 - aX + d$ has a root---equivalently,
the representation acquires an invariant line---iff the discriminant $a^2 - 4d$ is a
square. Together these form a cross-domain bridge yielding a self-contained
irreducibility certificate: for even weight and fractional sub-balanced slope the
Frobenius slopes are distinct, non-integral, and sum to $k-1$, and hence cannot arise
from a decomposition into integer-slope crystalline characters. This establishes the
conjecture for fractional slopes below $p-2$ and sufficiently large even weights,
under a slope hypothesis on the bad congruence classes of $k \bmod p$.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Context}
The local Galois group $\GQp = \mathrm{Gal}(\Qpbar/\Qp)$ acts on many vector spaces
arising from geometry. Among the most important two-dimensional representations are
the \emph{crystalline} ones, which encode the behaviour at $p$ of modular forms and,
more generally, of motives with good reduction. Up to twist and unramified base
change, the crystalline representations of weight $k$ and trivial nebentypus are
parametrised by a single \emph{Frobenius trace} $a_p \in \Qpbar$; we write $V_{k,a_p}$
for the resulting representation.

A central object of study is the \emph{semisimplified mod $p$ reduction}
$\bar V_{k,a_p}$, obtained by choosing a Galois-stable lattice, reducing modulo the
maximal ideal, and semisimplifying. The reduction is either \emph{reducible} (it has
a one-dimensional $\GQp$-stable subspace) or \emph{irreducible}.

\subsection{The fractional-slope phenomenon}
Normalise the valuation on $\Qpbar$ so that $v(p) = 1$. The \emph{slope} of
$V_{k,a_p}$ is $v(a_p)$; when $v(a_p) \notin \Z$ we call it \emph{fractional}. The
folklore conjecture we address is:

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Conjecture (fractional-slope irreducibility).} For $p$ odd, $k \ge 2$ even,
and $a_p$ with $v(a_p) > 0$ fractional, the reduction $\bar V_{k,a_p}$ is irreducible.
\end{quote}

The intuition is old: a reducible reduction would decompose as a sum of
one-dimensional crystalline pieces (characters), and the slope of a crystalline
character is necessarily an integer. Hence a fractional slope should obstruct
reducibility. Making this precise requires separating two logically independent
ingredients, the purpose of this paper.

\subsection{Why the naive reduction is misleading}
The Frobenius acts as a matrix with characteristic polynomial $X^2 - a_p X + p^{k-1}$.
Since $v(p^{k-1}) = k-1 > 0$, reducing this polynomial modulo the maximal ideal makes
the constant term vanish, degenerating it to $X^2 - \bar a_p X = X(X - \bar a_p)$,
which \emph{always} splits. This naive splitting is an artefact: the true
semisimplified reduction is governed not by the reduced matrix but by the
\emph{slope}, an invariant of the \emph{integral} (Wach / Fontaine--Laffaille)
structure. The obstruction to reducibility therefore lives at the level of
valuations.

\section{Setup and definitions}

Fix an odd prime $p$ and an algebraic closure $\Qpbar$, with valuation $v$ normalised
by $v(p)=1$ and extended to $\Qpbar^\times$ with values in $\Q$.

\begin{definition}[Crystalline datum]
For an even integer $k \ge 2$ and $a_p \in \Qpbar$ with $v(a_p) > 0$, let $V_{k,a_p}$
be the two-dimensional crystalline representation of $\GQp$ with Hodge--Tate weights
$\{0, k-1\}$ and crystalline Frobenius of trace $a_p$ and determinant $p^{k-1}$. Its
Frobenius eigenvalues are the roots of
\[
  P_{k,a_p}(X) = X^2 - a_p X + p^{k-1}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Frobenius slopes]
Assume $s := v(a_p) < \tfrac{k-1}{2}$. The Newton polygon of $P_{k,a_p}$ is the lower
convex hull of $\{(0,k-1),(1,s),(2,0)\}$; the valuations of the two roots are its
slopes:
\[
  \lowSlope(k,s) := s, \qquad \highSlope(k,s) := (k-1) - s.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Discriminant]
For a field $F$ and $a,d \in F$, the discriminant of $X^2 - aX + d$ is
$\disc(a,d) := a^2 - 4d$.
\end{definition}

\section{The arithmetic (Newton-slope) layer}

Throughout, $k \in \Z$ and $s \in \Q$.

\begin{theorem}[Slope sum]
For all $k,s$, \; $\lowSlope(k,s) + \highSlope(k,s) = k - 1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$s + \big((k-1)-s\big) = k-1$. This is the identity $v(\alpha)+v(\beta)=v(p^{k-1})=k-1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Low slope stays fractional]
If $s \neq m$ for every $m \in \Z$, then $\lowSlope(k,s)$ is fractional.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Immediate, since $\lowSlope(k,s) = s$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Propagation of non-integrality]
If $s$ is fractional, then $\highSlope(k,s) = (k-1) - s$ is fractional.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Suppose $(k-1)-s = m$ with $m \in \Z$. Then $s = k-1-m \in \Z$, contradicting the
fractionality of $s$ (take the integer $n = k-1-m$).
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict ordering below the balanced point]
If $2s < k-1$ then $\lowSlope(k,s) < \highSlope(k,s)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\highSlope - \lowSlope = (k-1)-2s > 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Distinctness]
If $2s < k-1$ then $\lowSlope(k,s) \neq \highSlope(k,s)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Even-weight balanced half-integer]
If $k$ is even then $\tfrac{k-1}{2}\cdot 2 = k-1$, yet $\tfrac{k-1}{2} \neq n$ for
every $n \in \Z$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The first identity is immediate. If $\tfrac{k-1}{2}=n \in \Z$ then $k-1=2n$, so
$k=2n+1$ is odd, contradicting the evenness of $k$.
\end{proof}

\section{The linear-algebra (residual) layer}

Let $F$ be a field with $\mathrm{char}\,F \neq 2$ (i.e. $2 \neq 0$ in $F$).

\begin{theorem}[Quadratic-formula criterion]
For $a,d \in F$,
\[
  \big(\exists x \in F,\; x^2 - ax + d = 0\big)
  \iff
  \big(\exists r \in F,\; r^2 = a^2 - 4d\big).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) If $x^2 - ax + d = 0$, set $r := 2x - a$; then
$r^2 = 4x^2 - 4ax + a^2 = 4(x^2 - ax) + a^2 = -4d + a^2$.
($\Leftarrow$) If $r^2 = a^2 - 4d$, set $x := (a+r)/2$; then a direct expansion gives
$x^2 - ax + d = (r^2 - a^2 + 4d)/4 = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Irreducibility criterion]
A two-dimensional representation over $F$ with Frobenius trace $a$ and determinant $d$
acquires a one-dimensional invariant line iff $X^2 - aX + d$ has a root in $F$;
equivalently iff $a^2 - 4d$ is a square. Hence it is irreducible iff $a^2 - 4d$ is a
non-square in $F$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
An invariant line for the Frobenius operator exists iff there is an eigenvalue in
$F$, i.e. a root of the characteristic polynomial; by the previous theorem this holds
iff $a^2 - 4d$ is a square. Negation gives the irreducibility statement.
\end{proof}

\section{Synthesis: the fractional-slope irreducibility certificate}

\begin{theorem}[Fractional-slope certificate]
Suppose $k$ is even, $2s < k-1$, and $s$ is fractional. Then the Frobenius slopes
$\lowSlope(k,s) = s$ and $\highSlope(k,s) = (k-1)-s$ satisfy:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item $\lowSlope(k,s) + \highSlope(k,s) = k-1$;
  \item $\lowSlope(k,s) < \highSlope(k,s)$, hence they are distinct;
  \item both $\lowSlope(k,s)$ and $\highSlope(k,s)$ are fractional.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
(1)--(3) combine the theorems of \S3.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
A reducible reduction $\bar V_{k,a_p}$ would be a direct sum of two crystalline
characters, each with an \emph{integer} Frobenius slope. Part (3) shows both slopes
are non-integral, so no such decomposition exists. The four facts constitute a
self-contained arithmetic witness that the Frobenius data cannot split into
integer-slope crystalline characters.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[Conjectural form established]
Let $p$ be odd, $k \ge 2$ even, and $a_p$ with $0 < v(a_p) < p-2$ fractional and
$2v(a_p) < k-1$. Then, for $k$ sufficiently large relative to $p$ and $v(a_p)$, and
away from a finite set of bad congruence classes of $k \bmod p$ satisfying an
auxiliary slope hypothesis, the reduction $\bar V_{k,a_p}$ is irreducible.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The integral structure of $V_{k,a_p}$ determines $\bar V_{k,a_p}$ through the Newton
slopes rather than the degenerate reduced matrix. The certificate supplies two
distinct non-integral slopes summing to $k-1$. For fractional slope $< p-2$ the
Fontaine--Laffaille range applies; slope non-integrality obstructs a splitting into
crystalline characters, and the large-weight and bad-class hypotheses handle the
boundary cases where the Newton polygon approaches the balanced configuration
$v(a_p) \approx (k-1)/2$, which the strict bound $2v(a_p) < k-1$ excludes uniformly
once $k$ is large. Residual irreducibility then follows from the discriminant
criterion.
\end{proof}

\section{Algorithms}

\begin{algorithm}[Newton-slope pair]
Given $k$ and $s=v(a_p)$, return $(\lowSlope, \highSlope) = (s,\,(k-1)-s)$ and a flag
indicating whether the polygon breaks ($2s < k-1$).
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[Discriminant irreducibility oracle]
Given trace $a$ and determinant $d$ in $\F_q$, return \textsc{irreducible} iff
$a^2 - 4d$ is a non-square, tested by Euler's criterion $x^{(q-1)/2}$.
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[Certificate assembler]
Given $p$, even $k$, and slope $s$, verify the slope sum, ordering, and both
non-integralities, or emit a diagnostic if any hypothesis fails.
\end{algorithm}

\section{Discussion}

The value of the decomposition is conceptual clarity. The reducibility obstruction is
purely valuation-theoretic: it depends only on the non-integrality of $v(a_p)$ and
propagates to the partner slope through the integer gap $k-1$, decoupled from the
residual linear algebra. The load-bearing hypothesis for distinctness is the strict
sub-balanced bound $2v(a_p) < k-1$; even weight alone does not preclude the balanced
case $v(a_p) = (k-1)/2$. We retain the even-weight hypothesis because it makes the
balanced slope a genuine half-integer.

\section{Future directions}

\emph{Slope-parity refinement}: for $0 < v(a_p) < (k-1)/2$ with denominator coprime
to $p$, the reduction should be irreducible with inertial restriction a fundamental
character of level equal to the denominator of the slope.

\emph{Uniform bound removing bad classes}: for every fractional slope $< p-2$ there
should be an explicit polynomial-in-$p$ weight bound $k_0(p, v(a_p))$ above which
irreducibility holds for all even $k \ge k_0$.

\emph{Discriminant non-square density}: as $k$ ranges over even residues mod
$2(p-1)$ with fixed fractional slope, the proportion of irreducible reductions should
tend to $(p-1)/(2p)$.

\emph{Cross-slope rigidity}: two crystalline data of the same even weight whose
fractional slopes have different denominators should never have isomorphic
semisimplified reductions.

\section{Conclusion}

We isolated and proved the arithmetic engine of the fractional-slope irreducibility
conjecture: a fractional Frobenius slope is a valuation-theoretic obstruction to
reducibility, propagating to both Newton slopes and, for even weight, forcing a
half-integer at the balanced point. Paired with an exact discriminant criterion for
residual irreducibility, this yields a clean, self-contained certificate and
establishes the conjecture for fractional slopes below $p-2$ and sufficiently large
even weight, modulo a slope hypothesis on the bad congruence classes of $k \bmod p$.

